window.TEXTS_DATA.push(...[{"id":500,"title":"The Secret of Willow Station","eyebrow":"Crime & Detective","level":"A2","levelClass":"a2","source":"Original graded reader · Cozy Storybook","readTime":"6 min","words":750,"preview":"Every day the last train leaves Willow Station at six. One evening, a new ticket appears for a train that does not exist.","paragraphs":["Maya loved Willow Station. It was a little railway station beside a river in the English countryside. There were only two platforms, a red clock, and a friendly orange cat called Pip. Every Friday, Maya waited there for her grandfather. He always arrived on the six o’clock train, carrying a newspaper and a small bag of sweets. On a rainy Friday in October, Maya found something unusual under a wooden bench: a blue train <g word=\"ticket\" ph=\"/ˈtɪkɪt/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"A printed or digital pass for a journey.\" ex=\"Maya bought a train ticket.\">ticket</g> with a silver star. The ticket said, “<g word=\"platform\" ph=\"/ˈplætfɔːm/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"The raised area beside a railway track.\" ex=\"We waited on the platform.\">Platform</g> Three, 6:07.” But Willow Station had only two platforms.","Maya took the ticket to Mr Bell, the stationmaster. He wore a green waistcoat and knew every train on the <g word=\"timetable\" ph=\"/ˈtaɪmteɪbəl/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"A list showing when trains or buses leave.\" ex=\"Check the timetable before travelling.\">timetable</g>. “Platform Three?” he asked. “That platform closed many years ago.” He looked at the star on the ticket and became very quiet. Pip jumped onto the desk and touched the ticket with his nose. “There is an old <g word=\"tunnel\" ph=\"/ˈtʌnəl/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"A passage built through or under something.\" ex=\"The train went into a dark tunnel.\">tunnel</g> behind the station,” Mr Bell said. “My father worked there when I was a boy. Perhaps he left something.” Maya asked if they could look, and Mr Bell smiled. “After the last train,” he said.","While they waited for the last train, Maya helped Mr Bell put the newspapers away. He showed her an old photograph of Willow Station. In the picture, the clock tower was smaller, and a narrow third platform stood behind the building. “My father took this photograph,” Mr Bell said. “He loved making things for children.” Maya looked closely. A little silver star was painted above the old platform. It looked exactly like the star on her ticket. She wanted to ask more questions, but the telephone rang and Mr Bell went to answer it.","Maya sat near the window with Pip. The cat watched the rain fall on the tracks. Maya turned the ticket over. On the back, in very small writing, were the words “Time is a door.” She tried to imagine what this meant. Perhaps the clock could open something. Perhaps the ticket was only a joke. Suddenly Pip jumped down and ran towards the clock tower. Maya followed him, but he stopped at the locked outside gate. Behind the gate was an old bell covered with leaves. Mr Bell came outside and found them there. “Nobody has used that bell for thirty years,” he said. He brushed off the leaves and smiled. “My father used to ring it when the children arrived.”","A small blue train appeared in the distance. Maya checked the timetable: six o’clock, exactly on time. She put the ticket safely in her pocket and decided to tell her grandfather everything. Whatever the mystery was, she did not want to solve it alone. She looked again at the clock. The long hand moved slowly towards twelve, and Pip waited beside her as if he knew what would happen next.","At six o’clock, Maya’s grandfather arrived. She told him about the ticket, and he offered to help. Together they walked behind the station, past wet leaves and a small stone wall. They found a locked wooden door beneath the old clock tower. Pip sat in front of it and meowed. Maya noticed that the lock was shaped like a silver star. She carefully placed the blue ticket into a narrow opening beside it. Click! The door moved. Everyone stared. A warm light came from inside the room.","The room was small and full of dust. In the middle stood a wonderful model railway, with tiny houses, trees, bridges and trains. One little station was called Willow. Beside it was a letter. Mr Bell read it aloud. His father had made the model for the village children during a difficult winter. He wanted everyone to have somewhere to play when the weather was bad. But the room had been forgotten after the old tunnel closed. Maya found a switch and turned it on. A tiny blue train began to move around the tracks.","The next week, Maya and Mr Bell invited the village to Willow Station. The old model railway was clean and bright again. Children came with their families; grandparents told stories about the trains they remembered. Maya put the blue ticket in a little glass frame by the door. Nobody knew who had placed it beneath the bench, but Mr Bell had an idea. He watched Pip sleeping next to the model railway and laughed. “Perhaps our station has a very clever cat,” he said. Maya looked at the red clock. It was exactly 6:07."],"glossary":[{"word":"platform","ph":"/ˈplætfɔːm/","type":"noun","def":"The raised area beside a railway track.","ex":"We waited on the platform."},{"word":"ticket","ph":"/ˈtɪkɪt/","type":"noun","def":"A printed or digital pass for a journey.","ex":"Maya bought a train ticket."},{"word":"timetable","ph":"/ˈtaɪmteɪbəl/","type":"noun","def":"A list showing when trains or buses leave.","ex":"Check the timetable before travelling."},{"word":"tunnel","ph":"/ˈtʌnəl/","type":"noun","def":"A passage built through or under something.","ex":"The train went into a dark tunnel."}],"questions":[{"q":"Why is the blue ticket strange?","keywords":["ticket","train","timetable"],"model":"The ticket is for a train that is not on the timetable.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : Why is the blue ticket strange?","frModel":"Réponse possible : The ticket is for a train that is not on the timetable."},{"q":"Who helps Maya find the hidden room?","keywords":["station"],"model":"Mr Bell and the station cat Pip help her.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : Who helps Maya find the hidden room?","frModel":"Réponse possible : Mr Bell and the station cat Pip help her."},{"q":"What do Maya and Mr Bell decide to do at the end?","keywords":["forgotten","share","stories"],"model":"They open the forgotten room to the town and share its stories.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : What do Maya and Mr Bell decide to do at the end?","frModel":"Réponse possible : They open the forgotten room to the town and share its stories."}],"writingPrompt":"Write 100–140 words about what happens after the end of “The Secret of Willow Station”. Use two words from the glossary.","writingHints":["At the end of the story…","The next morning…","I think the character would…"],"writingKeywords":["platform","ticket","timetable","whispered","signal"],"strategies":{"skim":{"feedback":"✅ La situation centrale de <em>The Secret of Willow Station</em> peut être résumée en une ou deux phrases.","pistes":["Qui sont les personnages principaux ?","Quel problème doivent-ils résoudre ?","Raconte le dénouement sans détailler toutes les étapes."]},"scan":{"feedback":"✅ Repérer les faits précis demande de chercher les mots-clés plutôt que de tout relire.","pistes":["Cherche un nom propre ou un lieu important.","Repère un événement qui change la situation.","Appuie ta réponse sur un détail précis du texte."]},"infer":{"feedback":"✅ Une bonne inférence s’appuie sur des indices observables dans le texte.","pistes":["What do Maya and Mr Bell decide to do at the end?","Quels comportements des personnages peuvent justifier ta réponse ?","Fais la différence entre ce qui est écrit et ce que tu déduis."]},"vocab":{"feedback":"✅ Le contexte aide à comprendre un mot inconnu.","pistes":["Observe les mots qui entourent le terme.","Vérifie ton hypothèse dans le glossaire.","Réutilise le mot dans une phrase personnelle."]},"struct":{"feedback":"✅ Ce récit possède une progression narrative complète.","pistes":["Identifie la situation initiale, le problème, la recherche et la résolution.","Explique comment le suspense se construit.","Le texte comporte 8 paragraphes : donne un court titre à chacun."]},"intent":{"feedback":"✅ Réfléchis au message que l’auteur souhaite transmettre.","pistes":["Quelle décision du personnage fait avancer le récit ?","Comment l’atmosphère évolue-t-elle ?","Quelle idée l’histoire te laisse-t-elle à la fin ?"]}},"longRead":true,"isOriginal":false},{"id":501,"title":"The Lighthouse Postcard","eyebrow":"Contemporary Fiction","level":"A2","levelClass":"a2","source":"Original graded reader · Cozy Storybook","readTime":"6 min","words":743,"preview":"A postcard arrives without a name. A seaside walk becomes a search for a forgotten message.","paragraphs":["Alfie spent the summer holidays with his aunt in a village by the sea. The village had a small harbour, colourful houses, and an old lighthouse on a high green hill. Every morning he walked to the bakery with his friend Nora. One day, they went into a second-hand bookshop because it started to rain. Alfie opened a book about ships and found a <g word=\"postcard\" ph=\"/ˈpəʊstkɑːd/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"A small card with a picture and a <g word=\"message\" ph=\"/ˈmesɪdʒ/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"Information communicated to another person.\" ex=\"She left a message on the table.\">message</g>.\" ex=\"He sent a postcard from the beach.\">postcard</g> between its pages. On the front was the lighthouse. On the back someone had written: “I kept my <g word=\"promise\" ph=\"/ˈprɒmɪs/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"A firm statement that you will do something.\" ex=\"He kept his promise to call.\">promise</g>. Look at the light.” There was no name or address.","Nora wanted to solve the mystery. “Maybe it is a message from a sailor,” she said. Alfie looked at the picture. The lighthouse looked older, and the path to the <g word=\"shore\" ph=\"/ʃɔː/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"The land along the edge of the sea or lake.\" ex=\"They walked along the shore.\">shore</g> was different. At the corner of the card there was a small drawing of a daisy. They asked the bookshop owner about the postcard. He remembered an elderly woman who had donated several books. Her name was Mrs Reed, and she used to work at the lighthouse. “You can visit her,” he said. “She lives above the harbour, near the blue gate.”","The two friends walked up the hill to the blue gate, but they stopped at the top of the path. A thick cloud covered the sun, and the lighthouse stood grey against the sky. Nora held up the old postcard. The picture showed three windows, although the lighthouse now had only two. “Someone changed the building,” she said. Alfie looked again and noticed a little path behind the <g word=\"keeper\" ph=\"/ˈkiːpə/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"A person who looks after a place or object.\" ex=\"The lighthouse keeper checked the lamp.\">keeper</g>'s house. They followed it to a garden full of white daisies. A wooden bench stood under a tree, and its paint was the same shade of blue as the gate.","A fisherman was working near a small shed. When they showed him the postcard, he remembered the old storm. “The light went out for only a few minutes,” he said, “but those minutes felt very long.” His name was Tom, and his father had sailed on the boat that reached the harbour that night. Tom did not know who had written the message. He gave the children two pieces of advice: ask someone who knew the lighthouse well, and never throw away a story because it seems small.","Before they knocked on Mrs Reed's door, Nora took out her notebook. They made a list of what they knew: the postcard was old, the drawing showed the lighthouse, there was a daisy in one corner, and someone had kept a promise. “A good mystery needs good questions,” Nora said. Alfie laughed. “Then we have lots of good questions.” He felt a little nervous because they were visiting a stranger, so they phoned his aunt and told her where they were. She knew Mrs Reed and said it was fine to visit. Then the children walked through the blue gate and rang the bell.","Mrs Reed opened her door with a friendly smile. She listened carefully and invited the children to sit in her kitchen. She recognised the postcard at once. When she was young, she had worked with her brother Sam at the lighthouse. During a terrible storm, the light had stopped working. Mrs Reed <g word=\"borrowed\" ph=\"/ˈbɒrəʊd/\" type=\"verb\" def=\"Used something that belongs to someone else and will be returned.\" ex=\"I borrowed a book from the library.\">borrowed</g> a lamp from a fisherman, climbed the stairs, and helped guide a boat towards the harbour. Sam was on that boat. Later, he made the postcard to thank her, but he was too shy to give it to her.","“Sam moved away years ago,” Mrs Reed said. “We still write letters, but I have never seen this card.” She looked happy and a little sad. Nora noticed the little daisy. “Why is this flower here?” she asked. Mrs Reed laughed. “When we were children, Sam always put daisies in my schoolbag.” Alfie offered to take the postcard to Sam, but Mrs Reed had a better idea. “He is visiting tomorrow,” she said. “Would you both come to lunch?”","The next day the sun shone. Sam arrived with a small bunch of white daisies. Mrs Reed put the postcard on the table. Her brother looked surprised, then smiled. “I wrote that after the storm,” he said. “I thought you knew.” Mrs Reed shook her head. “You never told me.” They laughed together and talked for hours. On their way home, Alfie and Nora stopped by the lighthouse. The lamp was shining even in the afternoon. Alfie realised that some messages can travel a very long way before they finally reach the right person."],"glossary":[{"word":"postcard","ph":"/ˈpəʊstkɑːd/","type":"noun","def":"A small card with a picture and a message.","ex":"He sent a postcard from the beach."},{"word":"shore","ph":"/ʃɔː/","type":"noun","def":"The land along the edge of the sea or lake.","ex":"They walked along the shore."},{"word":"keeper","ph":"/ˈkiːpə/","type":"noun","def":"A person who looks after a place or object.","ex":"The lighthouse keeper checked the lamp."},{"word":"message","ph":"/ˈmesɪdʒ/","type":"noun","def":"Information communicated to another person.","ex":"She left a message on the table."},{"word":"borrowed","ph":"/ˈbɒrəʊd/","type":"verb","def":"Used something that belongs to someone else and will be returned.","ex":"I borrowed a book from the library."},{"word":"promise","ph":"/ˈprɒmɪs/","type":"noun","def":"A firm statement that you will do something.","ex":"He kept his promise to call."}],"questions":[{"q":"What does Alfie find in the bookshop?","keywords":["finds","postcard","picture","lighthouse"],"model":"He finds a postcard with a picture of the lighthouse and no name.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : What does Alfie find in the bookshop?","frModel":"Réponse possible : He finds a postcard with a picture of the lighthouse and no name."},{"q":"Who is Mrs Reed?","keywords":["retired","lighthouse","keeper","knows","story"],"model":"Mrs Reed is the retired lighthouse keeper who knows the story behind the postcard.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : Who is Mrs Reed?","frModel":"Réponse possible : Mrs Reed is the retired lighthouse keeper who knows the story behind the postcard."},{"q":"How do the children deliver the message?","keywords":["postcard"],"model":"They read the postcard to Mr Reed and give it to him.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : How do the children deliver the message?","frModel":"Réponse possible : They read the postcard to Mr Reed and give it to him."}],"writingPrompt":"Write 100–140 words about what happens after the end of “The Lighthouse Postcard”. Use two words from the glossary.","writingHints":["At the end of the story…","The next morning…","I think the character would…"],"writingKeywords":["postcard","shore","keeper","message","borrowed"],"strategies":{"skim":{"feedback":"✅ La situation centrale de <em>The Lighthouse Postcard</em> peut être résumée en une ou deux phrases.","pistes":["Qui sont les personnages principaux ?","Quel problème doivent-ils résoudre ?","Raconte le dénouement sans détailler toutes les étapes."]},"scan":{"feedback":"✅ Repérer les faits précis demande de chercher les mots-clés plutôt que de tout relire.","pistes":["Cherche un nom propre ou un lieu important.","Repère un événement qui change la situation.","Appuie ta réponse sur un détail précis du texte."]},"infer":{"feedback":"✅ Une bonne inférence s’appuie sur des indices observables dans le texte.","pistes":["How do the children deliver the message?","Quels comportements des personnages peuvent justifier ta réponse ?","Fais la différence entre ce qui est écrit et ce que tu déduis."]},"vocab":{"feedback":"✅ Le contexte aide à comprendre un mot inconnu.","pistes":["Observe les mots qui entourent le terme.","Vérifie ton hypothèse dans le glossaire.","Réutilise le mot dans une phrase personnelle."]},"struct":{"feedback":"✅ Ce récit possède une progression narrative complète.","pistes":["Identifie la situation initiale, le problème, la recherche et la résolution.","Explique comment le suspense se construit.","Le texte comporte 8 paragraphes : donne un court titre à chacun."]},"intent":{"feedback":"✅ Réfléchis au message que l’auteur souhaite transmettre.","pistes":["Quelle décision du personnage fait avancer le récit ?","Comment l’atmosphère évolue-t-elle ?","Quelle idée l’histoire te laisse-t-elle à la fin ?"]}},"longRead":true,"isOriginal":false},{"id":502,"title":"The Midnight Library Cat","eyebrow":"Fantasy","level":"A2","levelClass":"a2","source":"Original graded reader · Cozy Storybook","readTime":"6 min","words":729,"preview":"A small cat knows a secret about the library clock. Ella has one night to discover it.","paragraphs":["Ella loved the library in her town. It was a tall brick building with a round clock above the door. Every Tuesday, she went there after school and chose a book. One cold evening, Ella stayed until the librarian turned off the lights. She put her book in her bag and went to the door. Something shiny was on the floor. It was a silver <g word=\"key\" ph=\"/kiː/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"A small object used for opening a lock.\" ex=\"Ella found a silver key.\">key</g> shaped like a little star. A black-and-white cat appeared from behind a chair. The cat looked at the key, then at Ella, and walked away.","“Wait!” Ella called softly. The cat stopped near the oldest <g word=\"shelves\" ph=\"/ʃelvz/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"Flat boards used to store or display things.\" ex=\"The books stood on wooden shelves.\">shelves</g>. Ella followed it and found a small door between two bookcases. A golden star was painted on the wood. She put the silver key into the lock. The door opened with a <g word=\"quiet\" ph=\"/ˈkwaɪət/\" type=\"adjective\" def=\"With little or no noise.\" ex=\"The library was quiet at night.\">quiet</g> sound. Behind it was a long room full of books, lamps and beautiful pictures. The walls were covered with stars. Ella could hear a clock ticking. She looked at her watch. It was almost <g word=\"midnight\" ph=\"/ˈmɪdnaɪt/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"Twelve o’clock at night.\" ex=\"The clock struck midnight.\">midnight</g>, although she had left school only an hour earlier.","Ella walked slowly along the strange room. Some books were very old, with leather covers and faded gold letters. Others looked new. There was a book about football, a cookbook with red flowers, and a comic about a brave mouse. Ella picked up a little blue book, and the black-and-white cat made a loud noise. She put it back at once. “All right,” she said. “I will be careful.” A clock on the wall ticked, but its hands did not move. Ella checked her phone. It still showed seven o’clock. The secret room seemed to have its own time.","At the far end stood a desk with three empty boxes. Above them was a sign: “FOUND, REMEMBERED, RETURNED.” Ella did not understand the words at first. Then she looked at the books. Many had names inside. Some contained birthday messages, drawings, and little photographs. They were not just library books. They belonged to people. The cat jumped onto the box marked FOUND and looked at Ella. “First, we find the owners,” she said. The cat moved to REMEMBERED. “Then, we help them remember?” It blinked twice. Ella smiled. Maybe the cat understood her.","She found a tiny notebook beside the desk. On its first page someone had written, “A story is happiest when it has a reader.” Ella thought about her grandfather, who often told stories from his childhood. He sometimes forgot people's names, but he never forgot how the stories made him feel. Ella decided that every book deserved a chance to find its reader again. She put the notebook in her pocket, ready to write down the names she found.","On a wooden table there was a note: “THE LOST BOOKS NEED A WAY HOME.” Next to it stood a row of books with names written inside the covers. Ella opened the first book. It belonged to Mr Patel, who lived near the park. The next book belonged to Lucy, a girl from Ella’s class. The books looked sad and dusty. Ella did not think books could feel sad, but this was a strange library. The cat jumped onto the table and pushed a book towards her. “You want me to <g word=\"return\" ph=\"/rɪˈtɜːn/\" type=\"verb\" def=\"To bring something back to its owner or place.\" ex=\"Please return the book next week.\">return</g> them?” Ella asked. The cat blinked.","Ella put three books into her schoolbag. The cat showed her another small door, and suddenly she was back in the normal library. Outside, the clock said seven o’clock. The next morning, Ella gave Mr Patel his old gardening book. He was delighted. She gave Lucy a picture book that Lucy had lost when she was six. Lucy hugged it and laughed. Ella returned the last book to the librarian, who looked at the silver key with great interest. “Where did you get this?” she whispered.","That evening, the librarian and Ella visited the secret room together. The black-and-white cat was waiting on the table. The librarian smiled. “I wondered when someone would find this place again.” She explained that the room was very old. It kept stories safe until their readers were ready to find them. From that day, Ella helped every Tuesday. Sometimes a book returned to its owner. Sometimes it found a new reader. At midnight, when the town was asleep, the little cat sat by the star door and listened to the sound of pages turning."],"glossary":[{"word":"shelves","ph":"/ʃelvz/","type":"noun","def":"Flat boards used to store or display things.","ex":"The books stood on wooden shelves."},{"word":"key","ph":"/kiː/","type":"noun","def":"A small object used for opening a lock.","ex":"Ella found a silver key."},{"word":"quiet","ph":"/ˈkwaɪət/","type":"adjective","def":"With little or no noise.","ex":"The library was quiet at night."},{"word":"midnight","ph":"/ˈmɪdnaɪt/","type":"noun","def":"Twelve o’clock at night.","ex":"The clock struck midnight."},{"word":"return","ph":"/rɪˈtɜːn/","type":"verb","def":"To bring something back to its owner or place.","ex":"Please return the book next week."}],"questions":[{"q":"What does Ella find under the library door?","keywords":["finds","silver","shaped"],"model":"She finds a tiny silver key shaped like a star.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : What does Ella find under the library door?","frModel":"Réponse possible : She finds a tiny silver key shaped like a star."},{"q":"Why is the secret room important?","keywords":["contains","books","people","forgot","return"],"model":"It contains books that people forgot to return and stories waiting to be shared.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : Why is the secret room important?","frModel":"Réponse possible : It contains books that people forgot to return and stories waiting to be shared."},{"q":"What does Ella choose to do?","keywords":["helps","return","books","tells","people"],"model":"She helps the cat return the books and tells people about the library.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : What does Ella choose to do?","frModel":"Réponse possible : She helps the cat return the books and tells people about the library."}],"writingPrompt":"Write 100–140 words about what happens after the end of “The Midnight Library Cat”. Use two words from the glossary.","writingHints":["At the end of the story…","The next morning…","I think the character would…"],"writingKeywords":["shelves","key","quiet","midnight","borrowed"],"strategies":{"skim":{"feedback":"✅ La situation centrale de <em>The Midnight Library Cat</em> peut être résumée en une ou deux phrases.","pistes":["Qui sont les personnages principaux ?","Quel problème doivent-ils résoudre ?","Raconte le dénouement sans détailler toutes les étapes."]},"scan":{"feedback":"✅ Repérer les faits précis demande de chercher les mots-clés plutôt que de tout relire.","pistes":["Cherche un nom propre ou un lieu important.","Repère un événement qui change la situation.","Appuie ta réponse sur un détail précis du texte."]},"infer":{"feedback":"✅ Une bonne inférence s’appuie sur des indices observables dans le texte.","pistes":["What does Ella choose to do?","Quels comportements des personnages peuvent justifier ta réponse ?","Fais la différence entre ce qui est écrit et ce que tu déduis."]},"vocab":{"feedback":"✅ Le contexte aide à comprendre un mot inconnu.","pistes":["Observe les mots qui entourent le terme.","Vérifie ton hypothèse dans le glossaire.","Réutilise le mot dans une phrase personnelle."]},"struct":{"feedback":"✅ Ce récit possède une progression narrative complète.","pistes":["Identifie la situation initiale, le problème, la recherche et la résolution.","Explique comment le suspense se construit.","Le texte comporte 8 paragraphes : donne un court titre à chacun."]},"intent":{"feedback":"✅ Réfléchis au message que l’auteur souhaite transmettre.","pistes":["Quelle décision du personnage fait avancer le récit ?","Comment l’atmosphère évolue-t-elle ?","Quelle idée l’histoire te laisse-t-elle à la fin ?"]}},"longRead":true,"isOriginal":false},{"id":503,"title":"The Rainy-Day Detective Club","eyebrow":"Crime & Detective","level":"A2","levelClass":"a2","source":"Original graded reader · Cozy Storybook","readTime":"6 min","words":716,"preview":"The school trophy disappears before the autumn fair. Three friends follow clues through the rain.","paragraphs":["It was the morning of the autumn school fair, and rain was falling on the playground. Amira, Ben and Lily were putting up paper stars in the school hall. On a table near the stage stood the golden <g word=\"trophy\" ph=\"/ˈtrəʊfi/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"An object given as a prize for winning.\" ex=\"The team won a golden trophy.\">trophy</g> for the class quiz. Or rather, it should have stood there. When Lily looked at the table, the trophy was gone. There was only a white square where it had been. “We need to find it before the fair begins,” said Amira. Ben took a notebook from his pocket. “This is a job for the Rainy-Day Detective Club!”","The three friends checked the doors. The front door was closed, and the side door was wet. Near it, they saw little muddy marks on the floor. Ben thought they were shoe prints. Lily bent down. “Those are too small,” she said. “Maybe they belong to an animal.” They followed the marks past the music room and into the corridor. They stopped near a coat rack. A blue <g word=\"raincoat\" ph=\"/ˈreɪnkəʊt/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"A coat that protects you from rain.\" ex=\"She wore a yellow raincoat.\">raincoat</g> was on the floor, and a little bell was caught in its pocket. Nobody knew why the bell was there.","Ben wanted to tell the head teacher that someone had stolen the trophy. Amira stopped him. “We don't know that,” she said. “We only know the trophy is missing.” Lily agreed. She had read enough detective stories to know that a <g word=\"clue\" ph=\"/kluː/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"A fact that helps solve a mystery.\" ex=\"The muddy footprint was a clue.\">clue</g> was not the same as proof. They decided to search carefully and write down what they found. Ben drew a little map of the hall, with the stage, the doors and the trophy table. Amira made a list of people who had entered the room, and Lily looked at the muddy marks again.","The rain was still falling. A teacher came in carrying umbrellas. “Why are you all on the floor?” she asked. They explained the problem. Instead of laughing, she helped them look under the tables. They found a paper star, a pencil, and two lost buttons, but no trophy. Near the window they discovered a piece of red ribbon. It could be part of a decoration, or it could be important. Ben put it beside the bell in his notebook. “Two clues!” he said. “Maybe,” Amira replied.","A group of younger pupils arrived to practise their song for the fair. The friends moved their search to the corridor so they would not disturb them. Amira noticed that the muddy marks changed direction near the coat rack. Whatever had made them had turned around. Lily picked up the little bell and shook it. It made a bright sound. From somewhere beyond the hall came a short bark. Everyone heard it, but the music started again before they could find where the sound came from. They agreed to ask the caretaker who was working in that part of the school.","Amira asked the caretaker if anyone had entered the hall. “Only teachers and Mr Chips,” he said. Mr Chips was the friendly school dog. He often slept in the office and liked carrying things in his mouth. The friends looked at one another. “Would a dog carry a heavy trophy?” Ben asked. “Perhaps if it had a handle,” Lily replied. They ran to the office, but Mr Chips was not there. On his bed was a piece of red ribbon from the fair decorations.","The children went back to the hall. The music teacher was practising a song, and the curtains on the stage were moving. Amira noticed a paw print near the steps. She pulled the <g word=\"curtain\" ph=\"/ˈkɜːtən/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"A piece of cloth used to cover or divide a space.\" ex=\"They looked behind the curtain.\">curtain</g> aside and laughed. Mr Chips was sleeping behind the stage, with the trophy next to his paws. He had taken it from the table and carried it to his favourite quiet place. The little bell belonged to his collar, which had come loose. Nobody was angry with him, because nothing was broken.","The teacher thanked the detectives. Before the fair, the children cleaned the trophy and placed it on a higher shelf. They made a new sign for Mr Chips: “OFFICIAL FAIR HELPER.” When the visitors arrived, the rain had stopped and the sun came through the windows. Amira, Ben and Lily watched the quiz begin. Their first case was solved, but Ben kept the notebook. “You never know,” he said. “The next mystery might happen on a sunny day.”"],"glossary":[{"word":"trophy","ph":"/ˈtrəʊfi/","type":"noun","def":"An object given as a prize for winning.","ex":"The team won a golden trophy."},{"word":"clue","ph":"/kluː/","type":"noun","def":"A fact that helps solve a mystery.","ex":"The muddy footprint was a clue."},{"word":"raincoat","ph":"/ˈreɪnkəʊt/","type":"noun","def":"A coat that protects you from rain.","ex":"She wore a yellow raincoat."},{"word":"curtain","ph":"/ˈkɜːtən/","type":"noun","def":"A piece of cloth used to cover or divide a space.","ex":"They looked behind the curtain."}],"questions":[{"q":"What disappears from the school hall?","keywords":["school","trophy","disappears","display","table"],"model":"The school fair trophy disappears from the display table.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : What disappears from the school hall?","frModel":"Réponse possible : The school fair trophy disappears from the display table."},{"q":"Which clue helps the friends?","keywords":["Small","muddy","prints","behind","stage"],"model":"Small muddy paw prints lead behind the stage curtain.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : Which clue helps the friends?","frModel":"Réponse possible : Small muddy paw prints lead behind the stage curtain."},{"q":"Who moved the trophy?","keywords":["school","carried","quiet","place","behind"],"model":"The school dog carried it to a quiet place behind the stage.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : Who moved the trophy?","frModel":"Réponse possible : The school dog carried it to a quiet place behind the stage."}],"writingPrompt":"Write 100–140 words about what happens after the end of “The Rainy-Day Detective Club”. Use two words from the glossary.","writingHints":["At the end of the story…","The next morning…","I think the character would…"],"writingKeywords":["trophy","clue","raincoat","puddle","curtain"],"strategies":{"skim":{"feedback":"✅ La situation centrale de <em>The Rainy-Day Detective Club</em> peut être résumée en une ou deux phrases.","pistes":["Qui sont les personnages principaux ?","Quel problème doivent-ils résoudre ?","Raconte le dénouement sans détailler toutes les étapes."]},"scan":{"feedback":"✅ Repérer les faits précis demande de chercher les mots-clés plutôt que de tout relire.","pistes":["Cherche un nom propre ou un lieu important.","Repère un événement qui change la situation.","Appuie ta réponse sur un détail précis du texte."]},"infer":{"feedback":"✅ Une bonne inférence s’appuie sur des indices observables dans le texte.","pistes":["Who moved the trophy?","Quels comportements des personnages peuvent justifier ta réponse ?","Fais la différence entre ce qui est écrit et ce que tu déduis."]},"vocab":{"feedback":"✅ Le contexte aide à comprendre un mot inconnu.","pistes":["Observe les mots qui entourent le terme.","Vérifie ton hypothèse dans le glossaire.","Réutilise le mot dans une phrase personnelle."]},"struct":{"feedback":"✅ Ce récit possède une progression narrative complète.","pistes":["Identifie la situation initiale, le problème, la recherche et la résolution.","Explique comment le suspense se construit.","Le texte comporte 8 paragraphes : donne un court titre à chacun."]},"intent":{"feedback":"✅ Réfléchis au message que l’auteur souhaite transmettre.","pistes":["Quelle décision du personnage fait avancer le récit ?","Comment l’atmosphère évolue-t-elle ?","Quelle idée l’histoire te laisse-t-elle à la fin ?"]}},"longRead":true,"isOriginal":false},{"id":504,"title":"The Clockmaker's Garden","eyebrow":"Fantasy","level":"B1","levelClass":"b1","source":"Original graded reader · Cozy Storybook","readTime":"7 min","words":845,"preview":"The village clocks have stopped. In a hidden greenhouse, mechanical birds seem to know why.","paragraphs":["In Bellmere, a village famous for its clocks, everyone knew the time without looking at a phone. There was a clock above the bakery, one on the school roof, and another in the market square. Every clock had been made or repaired by Mr Finch, the oldest clockmaker in the county. Then, on the first evening of spring, every clock stopped at exactly 7:13. They began working again the next morning, but the same thing happened every evening. At first people laughed. After a week, the baker was missing orders and the evening buses were leaving late. Lina, who lived above the bakery, decided to find out why.","She visited Mr Finch after school. His workshop smelled of warm wood and metal polish. The shelves were crowded with brass <g word=\"gears\" ph=\"/ɡɪəz/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"Toothed wheels that move together in a machine.\" ex=\"The gears turned slowly.\">gears</g>, little springs and clocks of every shape. “Is someone playing a trick?” Lina asked. Mr Finch was examining a pocket watch. “A trick, perhaps,” he said. “But not the sort you mean.” He showed her a picture of a <g word=\"greenhouse\" ph=\"/ˈɡriːnhaʊs/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"A glass building used for growing plants.\" ex=\"Flowers grew inside the greenhouse.\">greenhouse</g> behind his house. It had been closed for years because its glass roof needed <g word=\"repair\" ph=\"/rɪˈpeə/\" type=\"verb\" def=\"To make something work again.\" ex=\"We helped repair the clock.\">repair</g>. In the photograph, <g word=\"mechanical\" ph=\"/mɪˈkænɪkəl/\" type=\"adjective\" def=\"Working by means of machinery.\" ex=\"A mechanical bird sat on the branch.\">mechanical</g> birds perched among enormous flowers. Each bird had a small clock hidden inside its body. Lina thought the picture looked like an illustration from an old fairy tale.","The next morning Lina returned to the clock shop before school. She asked Mr Finch why the greenhouse had been locked for so long. He did not answer immediately. Instead, he placed three small gears on the workbench. One was made of brass, one of wood and one of silver. “Which one would you use?” he asked. Lina chose the strongest-looking gear, but Mr Finch shook his head. “A machine works when the parts cooperate,” he explained. “Strength matters less than the right fit.” He showed her how each wheel made another turn, until the smallest gear moved the hands of a clock.","Lina spent the afternoon reading the old repair notes. A few pages were missing, and several sketches showed birds with musical symbols beside them. She discovered that each mechanical bird had been tuned to a different note. Together they could play a melody that told the village clock when to ring. It sounded impossible, yet the diagrams were beautifully precise. Mr Finch admitted that he had stopped visiting the greenhouse after his sister, who had designed the birds, moved abroad. “I knew how to keep them clean,” he said, “but she knew how to make them sing.”","Lina wrote to his sister using the address at the back of the notebook. That evening a reply arrived. The message contained only a photograph and a sentence: “Begin with the robin; it always knows the <g word=\"rhythm\" ph=\"/ˈrɪðəm/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"A regular pattern of sounds or movement.\" ex=\"The drums kept a steady rhythm.\">rhythm</g>.” Lina showed Mr Finch the picture. He studied it for a long time. Then he found an old key under the till. They collected a toolbox, a lantern and a notebook and agreed to visit the greenhouse together. Lina hoped she would hear the birds sing, but she was also beginning to understand why the silence had lasted so long.","That evening Lina and Mr Finch entered the greenhouse. The air was warmer than outside, and tiny lights shone beneath the leaves. At seven o’clock, the metal birds began to sing, one after another. Each note was clear and sweet. Then the largest bird, a copper robin on a high branch, opened its beak but made no sound. All the other birds went quiet. A moment later the bells of the village clocks stopped. “They used to keep the village in rhythm,” Mr Finch explained. “My grandmother built them so that nobody would have to work alone to keep the clocks running.” He looked worried. He had forgotten how to repair the robin.","Lina returned the next day with her science notebook. She sketched the robin and watched how its gears moved. One tiny wheel was missing a tooth. Mr Finch said they would need a replacement, but none of his modern parts would fit. Lina looked carefully at the greenhouse floor. Under a flowerpot lay an old box containing spare wheels, each wrapped in paper. They tested three before finding the correct one. Mr Finch guided Lina’s hands as she fitted it into place. They checked the mechanism twice, then waited for evening. When the garden began to sing, the copper robin gave a beautiful final note. From the village came the sound of bells.","The next morning, people could hear the clocks again. Lina expected Mr Finch to lock the greenhouse, but he invited the school to visit it instead. Students learnt how gears worked, how to care for plants, and why the mechanical birds were designed to sing together. On Friday afternoons, Lina helped Mr Finch maintain the garden. One afternoon she asked him why the robin had stopped in the first place. Mr Finch thought for a while. “Even a carefully made machine needs someone to listen,” he said. Lina wrote the sentence in her notebook. It was the best lesson she had learnt all term."],"glossary":[{"word":"greenhouse","ph":"/ˈɡriːnhaʊs/","type":"noun","def":"A glass building used for growing plants.","ex":"Flowers grew inside the greenhouse."},{"word":"gears","ph":"/ɡɪəz/","type":"noun","def":"Toothed wheels that move together in a machine.","ex":"The gears turned slowly."},{"word":"repair","ph":"/rɪˈpeə/","type":"verb","def":"To make something work again.","ex":"We helped repair the clock."},{"word":"mechanical","ph":"/mɪˈkænɪkəl/","type":"adjective","def":"Working by means of machinery.","ex":"A mechanical bird sat on the branch."},{"word":"rhythm","ph":"/ˈrɪðəm/","type":"noun","def":"A regular pattern of sounds or movement.","ex":"The drums kept a steady rhythm."}],"questions":[{"q":"What happens to the clocks in Bellmere?","keywords":["village","clocks","every","evening"],"model":"All the village clocks stop at the same time every evening.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : What happens to the clocks in Bellmere?","frModel":"Réponse possible : All the village clocks stop at the same time every evening."},{"q":"What does Lina discover in the garden?","keywords":["discovers","mechanical","birds","village","clock"],"model":"She discovers that the mechanical birds keep the village clock connected to the greenhouse.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : What does Lina discover in the garden?","frModel":"Réponse possible : She discovers that the mechanical birds keep the village clock connected to the greenhouse."},{"q":"How is the problem solved?","keywords":["clockmaker","repair","music","mechanism","together"],"model":"The clockmaker and Lina repair the music mechanism together and restore the clocks.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : How is the problem solved?","frModel":"Réponse possible : The clockmaker and Lina repair the music mechanism together and restore the clocks."}],"writingPrompt":"Write 100–140 words about what happens after the end of “The Clockmaker's Garden”. Use two words from the glossary.","writingHints":["At the end of the story…","The next morning…","I think the character would…"],"writingKeywords":["greenhouse","gears","repair","mechanical","patient"],"strategies":{"skim":{"feedback":"✅ La situation centrale de <em>The Clockmaker&#x27;s Garden</em> peut être résumée en une ou deux phrases.","pistes":["Qui sont les personnages principaux ?","Quel problème doivent-ils résoudre ?","Raconte le dénouement sans détailler toutes les étapes."]},"scan":{"feedback":"✅ Repérer les faits précis demande de chercher les mots-clés plutôt que de tout relire.","pistes":["Cherche un nom propre ou un lieu important.","Repère un événement qui change la situation.","Appuie ta réponse sur un détail précis du texte."]},"infer":{"feedback":"✅ Une bonne inférence s’appuie sur des indices observables dans le texte.","pistes":["How is the problem solved?","Quels comportements des personnages peuvent justifier ta réponse ?","Fais la différence entre ce qui est écrit et ce que tu déduis."]},"vocab":{"feedback":"✅ Le contexte aide à comprendre un mot inconnu.","pistes":["Observe les mots qui entourent le terme.","Vérifie ton hypothèse dans le glossaire.","Réutilise le mot dans une phrase personnelle."]},"struct":{"feedback":"✅ Ce récit possède une progression narrative complète.","pistes":["Identifie la situation initiale, le problème, la recherche et la résolution.","Explique comment le suspense se construit.","Le texte comporte 8 paragraphes : donne un court titre à chacun."]},"intent":{"feedback":"✅ Réfléchis au message que l’auteur souhaite transmettre.","pistes":["Quelle décision du personnage fait avancer le récit ?","Comment l’atmosphère évolue-t-elle ?","Quelle idée l’histoire te laisse-t-elle à la fin ?"]}},"longRead":true,"isOriginal":false},{"id":505,"title":"The Museum After Closing","eyebrow":"Crime & Detective","level":"B1","levelClass":"b1","source":"Original graded reader · Cozy Storybook","readTime":"7 min","words":813,"preview":"A curious museum volunteer follows clues to a room that does not appear on the visitor map.","paragraphs":["Zara volunteered at the Town Museum every Saturday. She checked tickets, helped visitors find the dinosaurs and learnt surprising facts from the curators. One rainy evening, her friend Theo came to collect her after the museum closed. As they crossed the entrance hall, Zara noticed a narrow line of light behind a wall near the staircase. The visitor map showed only a storage cupboard in that part of the building. Yet the light was moving, as if somebody were carrying a lamp through a room. Zara called to Ms Vale, the <g word=\"curator\" ph=\"/kjʊəˈreɪtə/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"A person responsible for a museum or collection.\" ex=\"The curator organised an <g word=\"exhibition\" ph=\"/ˌeksɪˈbɪʃən/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"A public <g word=\"display\" ph=\"/dɪˈspleɪ/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"An arrangement of objects for people to see.\" ex=\"The fossils were on display.\">display</g> of interesting objects or artworks.\" ex=\"We visited the museum exhibition.\">exhibition</g>.\">curator</g>, who checked the <g word=\"corridor\" ph=\"/ˈkɒrɪdɔː/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"A long passage connecting rooms.\" ex=\"They walked down the corridor.\">corridor</g> and found nothing unusual. Before leaving, Zara took a photograph of the wall.","The following Saturday, Zara compared her photograph with an old museum guide from 1938. In the guide, the wall was a doorway leading to an exhibition called “Wings of Tomorrow”. No one at the desk had heard of it. Theo joined her in the archives, where they found newspaper articles about children who had designed mechanical butterflies during a difficult winter. The butterflies could move their wings using little gears and springs. The articles ended suddenly when the museum closed for repairs. Why had nobody opened the exhibition again? Zara began to think the moving light might be a reflection, but she still wanted to see what was behind the wall.","Zara and Theo came back to the museum the following Saturday. The afternoon was busy: a family looked at the fossils, two tourists admired the old coins, and a class of younger children drew pictures of a Roman bowl. Zara liked helping visitors, but she kept thinking about the unexplained light behind the exhibition wall. During her break she searched the museum catalogue. It contained hundreds of photographs, but very few were labelled with the names of the people who had made the objects.","In a box of old programmes, she found a page describing an exhibition called “Made Here.” It had featured useful things built by local residents: bicycles repaired from spare parts, musical instruments made in workshops, and carefully painted toys. One photograph showed a small mechanical bird on a wooden stand. Zara recognised the room behind it. The windows matched the ones she had seen through the gap in the wall. On the back of the photograph, in faded pencil, someone had written, “Please keep the makers' names.” Theo read the words twice. “Maybe the mystery isn't about a secret treasure,” he said. “Maybe it's about people.”","They made a list of questions for Ms Vale, the curator. Why had the exhibition been closed? Where were the original labels? Could the makers or their families still live nearby? Zara wanted to open the door at once, but Theo reminded her that museums had rules for a reason. Objects could be fragile, and nobody should enter a closed room without permission. They waited until Ms Vale had finished with a group of visitors. Then they showed her the old programme, the photograph and the note. She looked surprised but interested, and asked them to bring a torch.","Ms Vale agreed to investigate with them. She brought a key ring, a torch and an old floor plan. Behind a display case, the three found a narrow door that had been painted the same colour as the wall. It opened into a quiet room with glass cabinets and dusty posters. A huge mobile hung from the ceiling. When the torchlight touched it, dozens of little metal wings shone blue and gold. The children were amazed. A ventilation fan turned the mobile slowly, and its reflection danced on the wall outside. That was the mysterious light Zara had noticed.","The room was beautiful but needed careful work. Some butterflies were missing wings, while others were covered in dust. Ms Vale found the original names of the young artists in a notebook. Several of them were still living in the town, now much older. Zara and Theo helped write invitations asking them to share their memories. Over the next month, volunteers cleaned the cases, recorded interviews and <g word=\"restored\" ph=\"/rɪˈstɔːd/\" type=\"verb\" def=\"Returned something to good condition.\" ex=\"The painting was carefully restored.\">restored</g> the butterflies. One woman remembered making a bright green butterfly with her best friend when they were eleven. She became emotional when she saw it again, but her smile was enormous.","On the day the exhibition reopened, a small crowd gathered in the entrance hall. Zara and Theo stood beside the new sign. Ms Vale welcomed the visitors and thanked everyone who had helped. Above their heads, the mechanical butterflies turned in the sunlight. The oldest visitors recognised their own creations; the youngest asked how to make them. Zara realised that a museum was not only a place for ancient objects. It could also be a place where ordinary people's stories were kept safe until someone was ready to listen. Before going home, she checked the updated visitor map. This time, the little room was clearly marked."],"glossary":[{"word":"curator","ph":"/kjʊəˈreɪtə/","type":"noun","def":"A person responsible for a museum or collection.","ex":"The curator organised an exhibition."},{"word":"exhibition","ph":"/ˌeksɪˈbɪʃən/","type":"noun","def":"A public display of interesting objects or artworks.","ex":"We visited the museum exhibition."},{"word":"display","ph":"/dɪˈspleɪ/","type":"noun","def":"An arrangement of objects for people to see.","ex":"The fossils were on display."},{"word":"corridor","ph":"/ˈkɒrɪdɔː/","type":"noun","def":"A long passage connecting rooms.","ex":"They walked down the corridor."},{"word":"restored","ph":"/rɪˈstɔːd/","type":"verb","def":"Returned something to good condition.","ex":"The painting was carefully restored."}],"questions":[{"q":"What changes after the museum closes?","keywords":["light","appears","missing","visitor"],"model":"A light appears in a room that is missing from the visitor map.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : What changes after the museum closes?","frModel":"Réponse possible : A light appears in a room that is missing from the visitor map."},{"q":"What do the friends find?","keywords":["discover","exhibition","mechanical","butterflies","created"],"model":"They discover an old exhibition of mechanical butterflies created by local children.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : What do the friends find?","frModel":"Réponse possible : They discover an old exhibition of mechanical butterflies created by local children."},{"q":"What does the curator plan?","keywords":["plans","restore","reopen","exhibition","visitors"],"model":"She plans to restore and reopen the exhibition for visitors.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : What does the curator plan?","frModel":"Réponse possible : She plans to restore and reopen the exhibition for visitors."}],"writingPrompt":"Write 100–140 words about what happens after the end of “The Museum After Closing”. Use two words from the glossary.","writingHints":["At the end of the story…","The next morning…","I think the character would…"],"writingKeywords":["curator","exhibition","label","display","corridor"],"strategies":{"skim":{"feedback":"✅ La situation centrale de <em>The Museum After Closing</em> peut être résumée en une ou deux phrases.","pistes":["Qui sont les personnages principaux ?","Quel problème doivent-ils résoudre ?","Raconte le dénouement sans détailler toutes les étapes."]},"scan":{"feedback":"✅ Repérer les faits précis demande de chercher les mots-clés plutôt que de tout relire.","pistes":["Cherche un nom propre ou un lieu important.","Repère un événement qui change la situation.","Appuie ta réponse sur un détail précis du texte."]},"infer":{"feedback":"✅ Une bonne inférence s’appuie sur des indices observables dans le texte.","pistes":["What does the curator plan?","Quels comportements des personnages peuvent justifier ta réponse ?","Fais la différence entre ce qui est écrit et ce que tu déduis."]},"vocab":{"feedback":"✅ Le contexte aide à comprendre un mot inconnu.","pistes":["Observe les mots qui entourent le terme.","Vérifie ton hypothèse dans le glossaire.","Réutilise le mot dans une phrase personnelle."]},"struct":{"feedback":"✅ Ce récit possède une progression narrative complète.","pistes":["Identifie la situation initiale, le problème, la recherche et la résolution.","Explique comment le suspense se construit.","Le texte comporte 8 paragraphes : donne un court titre à chacun."]},"intent":{"feedback":"✅ Réfléchis au message que l’auteur souhaite transmettre.","pistes":["Quelle décision du personnage fait avancer le récit ?","Comment l’atmosphère évolue-t-elle ?","Quelle idée l’histoire te laisse-t-elle à la fin ?"]}},"longRead":true,"isOriginal":false},{"id":506,"title":"The Island of Paper Birds","eyebrow":"Fantasy","level":"B1","levelClass":"b1","source":"Original graded reader · Cozy Storybook","readTime":"6 min","words":808,"preview":"Every paper bird that Faye folds flies away. One returns with an impossible invitation.","paragraphs":["Faye made paper birds whenever she felt nervous. She <g word=\"folded\" ph=\"/ˈfəʊldɪd/\" type=\"verb\" def=\"Bent paper or cloth over itself.\" ex=\"Faye folded the paper into a bird.\">folded</g> them on buses, in waiting rooms, and at the back of her school notebook. Most people thought the birds were pretty, but Faye knew something they did not: at sunset, the birds sometimes moved. A blue bird had once turned its head. A yellow one had hopped across her desk. One evening she folded a red bird with careful wings, and it flew through the open window. The next morning it returned carrying a tiny invitation written in silver ink: “The <g word=\"island\" ph=\"/ˈaɪlənd/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"An area of land surrounded by water.\" ex=\"They lived on a small island.\">Island</g> of Paper Birds needs a careful pair of hands.”","Faye followed the bird through the town and onto a hill above the sea. At the top stood an enormous paper arch that had never been there before. She touched its edge, felt a warm breeze, and stepped through. The world on the other side was filled with floating islands. Waterfalls fell into the clouds. Paper birds carried seeds between gardens suspended in the sky. A boy named Ren greeted her on a wooden bridge. He explained that the islands were connected by the Great Kite, a huge woven shape that caught the winds and helped the islands stay together. The Kite was beginning to tear, and every day the islands <g word=\"drifted\" ph=\"/ˈdrɪftɪd/\" type=\"verb\" def=\"Moved slowly with the wind or water.\" ex=\"The boat drifted across the lake.\">drifted</g> farther apart.","Faye sat with Ren on the bridge and watched three paper birds circle above the houses. The smallest bird landed on her hand. Its wings were torn, but it still tried to fly. “We have many brave birds,” Ren said, “but the wind is getting stronger.” Across the water Faye could see another island with little blue roofs. Every time the wind blew, the gap between the islands seemed wider. Ren explained that people had stopped visiting one another because the crossings were becoming dangerous. Friends could only wave from far away.","Faye wanted to help, but she was not sure she could. She made small birds in her notebook, not giant kites that held islands together. Ren took her to a square where people were folding strips of paper. There were old people, children, sailors and gardeners. Everybody knew a different way to fold. Some shapes were strong but heavy. Others were light but tore easily. Faye noticed that the strongest paper was made from several thin sheets crossed in different directions. “Could we try that with the Kite?” she asked. Ren's eyes lit up.","They spent the afternoon making small models. Their first attempt folded in the middle. The second floated for a moment, then broke. Faye felt frustrated. Back at school she often stopped trying when something went wrong. Here the islanders simply picked up the pieces and tried another idea. An elderly woman gave her a cup of warm fruit tea. “A good design is a conversation,” she said. “Every mistake tells you something.” Faye looked at the children working together and thought of the students in her class. Perhaps she had been too afraid to let other people see her mistakes. She opened her notebook and made a new drawing.","Ren led Faye to the workshop. It was full of paper, thread, bamboo, and half-finished designs. The islanders had tried to repair the Kite, but their patches kept breaking. They expected Faye to make something magical. She was frightened; she had never repaired anything so large. Then she noticed that the damaged sections were all folded in the same direction. Back home, her teacher had shown her that a strong model often needed folds going different ways. She suggested making a new <g word=\"pattern\" ph=\"/ˈpætən/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"A design or sequence that repeats in a predictable way.\" ex=\"They noticed a clear pattern.\">pattern</g>, with layers crossing one another like a woven basket.","The islanders worked together through the afternoon. Ren carried strips of paper; an elderly woman measured the bamboo; children folded thousands of small triangles. Faye showed them how to test the strength of each new piece. Sometimes the paper tore and they had to start again. Faye was embarrassed when one of her first patches failed, but Ren reminded her that learning was part of making. At last the Great Kite was ready. They raised it above the island and held their breath. Wind filled its colourful wings, the ropes became tight, and the islands stopped drifting. From every garden came cheering.","Before Faye returned home, Ren gave her a new notebook filled with beautiful folding patterns. He told her that the islanders had needed more than a magical bird: they needed someone who could notice a problem and help people work together. That evening Faye found herself back on the hill. The red bird rested on her shoulder for a moment, then joined its friends in the sunset. At school the following week, Faye started a paper-making club. The students made decorations for the library, but they also made something else: a little place where everyone could try, fail, and try again without feeling afraid."],"glossary":[{"word":"folded","ph":"/ˈfəʊldɪd/","type":"verb","def":"Bent paper or cloth over itself.","ex":"Faye folded the paper into a bird."},{"word":"pattern","ph":"/ˈpætən/","type":"noun","def":"A design or sequence that repeats in a predictable way.","ex":"They noticed a clear pattern."},{"word":"island","ph":"/ˈaɪlənd/","type":"noun","def":"An area of land surrounded by water.","ex":"They lived on a small island."},{"word":"drifted","ph":"/ˈdrɪftɪd/","type":"verb","def":"Moved slowly with the wind or water.","ex":"The boat drifted across the lake."}],"questions":[{"q":"What happens when Faye folds a paper bird?","keywords":["paper","comes","flies"],"model":"The paper bird comes to life and flies away.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : What happens when Faye folds a paper bird?","frModel":"Réponse possible : The paper bird comes to life and flies away."},{"q":"Why are the islands moving?","keywords":["great","keeps","together","begun"],"model":"The great kite that keeps them together has begun to tear.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : Why are the islands moving?","frModel":"Réponse possible : The great kite that keeps them together has begun to tear."},{"q":"How does Faye help?","keywords":["strong","folds","teaches","everyone","repair"],"model":"She uses strong new folds and teaches everyone to repair the kite together.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : How does Faye help?","frModel":"Réponse possible : She uses strong new folds and teaches everyone to repair the kite together."}],"writingPrompt":"Write 100–140 words about what happens after the end of “The Island of Paper Birds”. Use two words from the glossary.","writingHints":["At the end of the story…","The next morning…","I think the character would…"],"writingKeywords":["folded","fragile","pattern","island","drifted"],"strategies":{"skim":{"feedback":"✅ La situation centrale de <em>The Island of Paper Birds</em> peut être résumée en une ou deux phrases.","pistes":["Qui sont les personnages principaux ?","Quel problème doivent-ils résoudre ?","Raconte le dénouement sans détailler toutes les étapes."]},"scan":{"feedback":"✅ Repérer les faits précis demande de chercher les mots-clés plutôt que de tout relire.","pistes":["Cherche un nom propre ou un lieu important.","Repère un événement qui change la situation.","Appuie ta réponse sur un détail précis du texte."]},"infer":{"feedback":"✅ Une bonne inférence s’appuie sur des indices observables dans le texte.","pistes":["How does Faye help?","Quels comportements des personnages peuvent justifier ta réponse ?","Fais la différence entre ce qui est écrit et ce que tu déduis."]},"vocab":{"feedback":"✅ Le contexte aide à comprendre un mot inconnu.","pistes":["Observe les mots qui entourent le terme.","Vérifie ton hypothèse dans le glossaire.","Réutilise le mot dans une phrase personnelle."]},"struct":{"feedback":"✅ Ce récit possède une progression narrative complète.","pistes":["Identifie la situation initiale, le problème, la recherche et la résolution.","Explique comment le suspense se construit.","Le texte comporte 8 paragraphes : donne un court titre à chacun."]},"intent":{"feedback":"✅ Réfléchis au message que l’auteur souhaite transmettre.","pistes":["Quelle décision du personnage fait avancer le récit ?","Comment l’atmosphère évolue-t-elle ?","Quelle idée l’histoire te laisse-t-elle à la fin ?"]}},"longRead":true,"isOriginal":false},{"id":507,"title":"The Blue Door on Baker Street","eyebrow":"Fantasy","level":"B1","levelClass":"b1","source":"Original graded reader · Cozy Storybook","readTime":"6 min","words":767,"preview":"A bright blue door appears on a grey London street. It offers each visitor one unusual choice.","paragraphs":["Noah took the same bus to school every morning. On the way home, he passed a row of <g word=\"ordinary\" ph=\"/ˈɔːdɪnəri/\" type=\"adjective\" def=\"Usual, not unusual or special.\" ex=\"It looked like an ordinary shop.\">ordinary</g> shops on Baker Street: a bakery, a shoe repair shop, and a newsagent. Between the last two buildings stood a plain brick wall. One Thursday afternoon, Noah noticed something impossible. A bright blue door had appeared in the wall, with a brass handle shaped like a feather. No sign explained what was inside. People walked past without looking at it, as though it had always been there. Noah hesitated, then knocked three times.","An elderly woman opened the door. She wore round glasses and a purple coat. “Welcome to the House of Lost Sounds,” she said. Inside, narrow shelves held hundreds of tiny glass bottles. Some glowed like fireflies; others were dark and quiet. The woman explained that each <g word=\"bottle\" ph=\"/ˈbɒtəl/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"A container for liquids or other things.\" ex=\"She held a tiny blue bottle.\">bottle</g> held a sound someone had forgotten. Noah lifted a green bottle and heard the rain falling on a summer tent. A golden bottle contained a baby's first laugh. He listened to a silver one and heard the noise of a crowded railway station. Each sound made him think of a different memory.","Noah walked around the shop, trying to understand how the little bottles worked. Each had a handwritten label, but the labels described feelings rather than dates: JOY, COURAGE, HOME, HOPE. A narrow shelf held empty bottles waiting to be filled. The shopkeeper explained that the memories were not stolen from anyone. They were sounds that people had chosen to share: a grandmother's song, the whistle of a train, a family laughing at dinner. “The sound isn't the whole memory,” she said. “It only opens a door to it.”","Noah listened to a bottle marked HOME. He heard a gate opening, footsteps on wooden stairs and the soft voice of someone saying, “You're back.” The sound reminded him of his grandfather, who had moved to a care home the previous winter. When Noah visited, his grandfather sometimes forgot what day it was, but he always recognised the old music they played together. Another bottle held the sound of a busy street market. A third contained silence, and Noah discovered that silence could be comforting too.","He asked whether he could take a bottle for his grandfather. The shopkeeper nodded, but she warned him that he could choose only once. Noah considered taking a song they both knew. Then he remembered the sound of the wooden porch swing at his grandfather's old house. They had spent whole afternoons there, talking about everything and nothing. He could almost hear the squeak of the chain. The shopkeeper invited him to sit and think. She did not rush him, and the small shop grew peaceful while rain tapped on the windows. Noah closed his eyes and tried to <g word=\"remember\" ph=\"/rɪˈmembə/\" type=\"verb\" def=\"To bring something back into your mind.\" ex=\"I remember that song.\">remember</g> one sound that could make his grandfather smile.","“You may take one bottle,” the woman said. “But you must choose carefully. The sound should mean something to you.” Noah thought about his family. His grandfather loved jazz music. His sister enjoyed singing in the kitchen. But there was one sound he missed more than anything: his grandmother's laugh. She had moved to a care home far away, and Noah sometimes struggled to remember exactly what her laughter sounded like. He asked if such a bottle existed. The woman searched the shelves and brought him a little amber bottle with a paper label. There was no writing on it.","Noah held the bottle close to his ear. At first he heard the clink of teaspoons and the soft sound of a radio. Then came a laugh, bright and sudden, followed by his grandmother saying, “Another biscuit, Noah?” It was exactly as he remembered. Tears came to his eyes, but he was smiling. “Can I keep it forever?” he asked. The woman shook her head. “Sounds are meant to be shared. If you keep it hidden, it will slowly fade.” Noah understood. He thanked her and stepped back onto the street. The blue door closed quietly behind him.","That evening, Noah played the sound for his family. His father recognised his mother's laugh immediately and began telling stories about her. They phoned her the next day and laughed together for a long time. Over the weekend Noah returned to Baker Street, hoping to thank the shopkeeper again, but the wall was plain brick. There was no blue door. He was disappointed until he noticed something written in tiny letters on the corner of his amber bottle: “Remember by sharing.” Noah placed it on the mantelpiece, where everyone could hear it. The bottle never faded."],"glossary":[{"word":"ordinary","ph":"/ˈɔːdɪnəri/","type":"adjective","def":"Usual, not unusual or special.","ex":"It looked like an ordinary shop."},{"word":"bottle","ph":"/ˈbɒtəl/","type":"noun","def":"A container for liquids or other things.","ex":"She held a tiny blue bottle."},{"word":"remember","ph":"/rɪˈmembə/","type":"verb","def":"To bring something back into your mind.","ex":"I remember that song."}],"questions":[{"q":"Why is the blue door unusual?","keywords":["appears","place","where","there","brick"],"model":"It appears in a place where there was only a brick wall before.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : Why is the blue door unusual?","frModel":"Réponse possible : It appears in a place where there was only a brick wall before."},{"q":"What does the shopkeeper offer?","keywords":["offers","visitors","bottle","containing","sound"],"model":"She offers visitors a bottle containing a sound they have forgotten.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : What does the shopkeeper offer?","frModel":"Réponse possible : She offers visitors a bottle containing a sound they have forgotten."},{"q":"What does Noah choose?","keywords":["chooses","recording","grandmother","laughing","later"],"model":"He chooses a recording of his grandmother laughing and later shares it with his family.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : What does Noah choose?","frModel":"Réponse possible : He chooses a recording of his grandmother laughing and later shares it with his family."}],"writingPrompt":"Write 100–140 words about what happens after the end of “The Blue Door on Baker Street”. Use two words from the glossary.","writingHints":["At the end of the story…","The next morning…","I think the character would…"],"writingKeywords":["ordinary","doorway","visitor","bottle","remember"],"strategies":{"skim":{"feedback":"✅ La situation centrale de <em>The Blue Door on Baker Street</em> peut être résumée en une ou deux phrases.","pistes":["Qui sont les personnages principaux ?","Quel problème doivent-ils résoudre ?","Raconte le dénouement sans détailler toutes les étapes."]},"scan":{"feedback":"✅ Repérer les faits précis demande de chercher les mots-clés plutôt que de tout relire.","pistes":["Cherche un nom propre ou un lieu important.","Repère un événement qui change la situation.","Appuie ta réponse sur un détail précis du texte."]},"infer":{"feedback":"✅ Une bonne inférence s’appuie sur des indices observables dans le texte.","pistes":["What does Noah choose?","Quels comportements des personnages peuvent justifier ta réponse ?","Fais la différence entre ce qui est écrit et ce que tu déduis."]},"vocab":{"feedback":"✅ Le contexte aide à comprendre un mot inconnu.","pistes":["Observe les mots qui entourent le terme.","Vérifie ton hypothèse dans le glossaire.","Réutilise le mot dans une phrase personnelle."]},"struct":{"feedback":"✅ Ce récit possède une progression narrative complète.","pistes":["Identifie la situation initiale, le problème, la recherche et la résolution.","Explique comment le suspense se construit.","Le texte comporte 8 paragraphes : donne un court titre à chacun."]},"intent":{"feedback":"✅ Réfléchis au message que l’auteur souhaite transmettre.","pistes":["Quelle décision du personnage fait avancer le récit ?","Comment l’atmosphère évolue-t-elle ?","Quelle idée l’histoire te laisse-t-elle à la fin ?"]}},"longRead":true,"isOriginal":false},{"id":508,"title":"The Last Observatory","eyebrow":"Science Fiction","level":"B2","levelClass":"b2","source":"Original graded reader · Cozy Storybook","readTime":"6 min","words":908,"preview":"High in the mountains, a student astronomer begins receiving a message that may not be from space at all.","paragraphs":["The <g word=\"observatory\" ph=\"/əbˈzɜːvətri/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"A place with equipment used to study the sky.\" ex=\"The observatory had a huge telescope.\">observatory</g> stood above the clouds, where winter nights were almost completely silent. Hana had won a week-long science placement there, and she intended to make every minute count. She had spent years reading about distant planets; now she could work beside real astronomers and use instruments that filled an entire building. On her second evening, Dr Ellis asked her to check data from an old radio receiver. Most of the screen displayed ordinary background noise. Then, at exactly midnight, a narrow band of light appeared. It lasted seven seconds before vanishing. The next night, the same band returned at precisely the same time.","At breakfast, Hana explained her <g word=\"discovery\" ph=\"/dɪˈskʌvəri/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"Finding or learning something new.\" ex=\"It was an important discovery.\">discovery</g> with barely controlled excitement. The other students immediately began talking about messages from another civilisation. Dr Ellis listened without interrupting. When they finished, she asked a question that silenced the table: “What <g word=\"evidence\" ph=\"/ˈevɪdəns/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"Facts that support or disprove an explanation.\" ex=\"Scientists looked for evidence.\">evidence</g> would persuade you that it is not a message from space?” Hana realised she had been searching for reasons to believe her favourite explanation. She had not yet tried to disprove it. That afternoon she compared the signal with satellite schedules, weather reports and the position of the stars. Nothing matched. Yet the regular timing troubled her: nature rarely pays attention to the exact minute.","Hana began a research log in which every observation had two columns: what the instruments recorded and what she thought the records might mean. It was harder than she expected. A low tone seemed like a message when she wanted to find one, but the same tone could also come from an ordinary piece of equipment. Dr Ellis asked her to write down three possible explanations before choosing a favourite. Hana listed a distant source in space, an unusual effect of the weather, and interference from something close to the observatory. The final possibility felt disappointingly ordinary, but she kept it on the page.","During the afternoon they examined the telescope's cables and the old radio receiver in the basement. Hana discovered that the receiver had been repaired decades earlier by an apprentice whose name was no longer in the official records. The repair notes contained a sketch of a repeating signal. It looked similar to the <g word=\"pattern\" ph=\"/ˈpætən/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"A design or sequence that repeats in a predictable way.\" ex=\"They noticed a clear pattern.\">pattern</g> they had recorded that morning. She wondered whether the old equipment could be creating a reflection of its own transmission. Dr Ellis praised the question rather than the answer. “Good science is not a race to be right,” she said. “It is a method for becoming less wrong.”","That evening, clouds gathered around the mountain peaks. The technicians decided to prepare emergency power in case the weather became dangerous. Hana helped label batteries and check the torches. She felt disappointed that they might have to stop observing the sky, yet she was also curious about how the station worked when the main systems failed. From the control-room window she watched the final pink light disappear behind the mountains. She opened her notebook to a new page and wrote, “What changes when the power is off?”","That evening, the observatory's electricity failed during a snowstorm. The main telescope stopped moving and the heating system switched to emergency power. Hana helped carry battery lamps to the control room while Dr Ellis contacted the mountain rescue office. They were safe inside, but the disruption threatened to destroy a week's observations. As they waited, Hana noticed a small notebook in a drawer beneath the receiver. It belonged to a technician who had worked at the observatory thirty years earlier. On one page he had sketched a signal almost identical to hers, alongside the words “Old ridge station — transmitter forgotten?”","The next morning the weather improved. Dr Ellis contacted the local university, which still had the records of a closed weather station on the neighbouring ridge. It had once measured cloud cover, wind and temperature every night. A solar-powered transmitter had been left behind when the station shut down. During the winter its battery barely charged, so it sent its stored data in short bursts whenever enough energy returned. The seven-second signal was not a greeting from distant stars. It was a fragment of forgotten research, repeated again and again. Hana felt foolish for a moment, then strangely delighted.","Rather than hiding the mistake, Hana wrote a report explaining how they had identified the transmission. Her account included the theories they had rejected, the calculations that had failed, and the notebook that had finally pointed them towards the ridge. Dr Ellis suggested that she present it to the younger students. “Good science is a conversation with uncertainty,” she said. “The answer is rarely improved by pretending you knew it all along.” On Hana's final night, the aurora spread green and violet light across the mountain. She stepped outside to watch it, feeling smaller than ever beneath the sky and more curious than she had been on arrival.","Months later, Hana received a parcel from the observatory. Inside was a copy of the old technician's notebook and a letter explaining that the weather station data had helped researchers reconstruct a rare mountain storm. The forgotten signal had become useful after all. Hana pinned the letter above her desk. Whenever she felt too certain of an idea, she looked at it and remembered the question Dr Ellis had asked at breakfast. It was not the sort of discovery that made headlines, but it had changed the way Hana thought about the universe—and about her own place within it."],"glossary":[{"word":"observatory","ph":"/əbˈzɜːvətri/","type":"noun","def":"A place with equipment used to study the sky.","ex":"The observatory had a huge telescope."},{"word":"pattern","ph":"/ˈpætən/","type":"noun","def":"A design or sequence that repeats in a predictable way.","ex":"They noticed a clear pattern."},{"word":"evidence","ph":"/ˈevɪdəns/","type":"noun","def":"Facts that support or disprove an explanation.","ex":"Scientists looked for evidence."},{"word":"discovery","ph":"/dɪˈskʌvəri/","type":"noun","def":"Finding or learning something new.","ex":"It was an important discovery."}],"questions":[{"q":"Why does Hana doubt the strange signal?","keywords":["timing","follows","human","schedule","suggesting"],"model":"Its timing follows a human schedule, suggesting a source on Earth rather than in space.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : Why does Hana doubt the strange signal?","frModel":"Réponse possible : Its timing follows a human schedule, suggesting a source on Earth rather than in space."},{"q":"What is the real source of the signal?","keywords":["decades","mountain","weather","station","transmitting"],"model":"A decades-old mountain weather station transmitting stored observations.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : What is the real source of the signal?","frModel":"Réponse possible : A decades-old mountain weather station transmitting stored observations."},{"q":"What does Hana learn about discovery?","keywords":["learns","careful","verification","collaboration","matter"],"model":"She learns that careful verification and collaboration matter more than an exciting first explanation.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : What does Hana learn about discovery?","frModel":"Réponse possible : She learns that careful verification and collaboration matter more than an exciting first explanation."}],"writingPrompt":"Write 100–140 words about what happens after the end of “The Last Observatory”. Use two words from the glossary.","writingHints":["At the end of the story…","The next morning…","I think the character would…"],"writingKeywords":["observatory","frequency","pattern","evidence","silence"],"strategies":{"skim":{"feedback":"✅ La situation centrale de <em>The Last Observatory</em> peut être résumée en une ou deux phrases.","pistes":["Qui sont les personnages principaux ?","Quel problème doivent-ils résoudre ?","Raconte le dénouement sans détailler toutes les étapes."]},"scan":{"feedback":"✅ Repérer les faits précis demande de chercher les mots-clés plutôt que de tout relire.","pistes":["Cherche un nom propre ou un lieu important.","Repère un événement qui change la situation.","Appuie ta réponse sur un détail précis du texte."]},"infer":{"feedback":"✅ Une bonne inférence s’appuie sur des indices observables dans le texte.","pistes":["What does Hana learn about discovery?","Quels comportements des personnages peuvent justifier ta réponse ?","Fais la différence entre ce qui est écrit et ce que tu déduis."]},"vocab":{"feedback":"✅ Le contexte aide à comprendre un mot inconnu.","pistes":["Observe les mots qui entourent le terme.","Vérifie ton hypothèse dans le glossaire.","Réutilise le mot dans une phrase personnelle."]},"struct":{"feedback":"✅ Ce récit possède une progression narrative complète.","pistes":["Identifie la situation initiale, le problème, la recherche et la résolution.","Explique comment le suspense se construit.","Le texte comporte 9 paragraphes : donne un court titre à chacun."]},"intent":{"feedback":"✅ Réfléchis au message que l’auteur souhaite transmettre.","pistes":["Quelle décision du personnage fait avancer le récit ?","Comment l’atmosphère évolue-t-elle ?","Quelle idée l’histoire te laisse-t-elle à la fin ?"]}},"longRead":true,"isOriginal":false},{"id":509,"title":"The City Beneath the Tide","eyebrow":"Fantasy","level":"B2","levelClass":"b2","source":"Original graded reader · Cozy Storybook","readTime":"6 min","words":903,"preview":"At the lowest tide of the century, a town discovers stone streets and a history the maps have forgotten.","paragraphs":["At dawn, the sea withdrew farther than anyone in Gullhaven had ever seen. Boats rested on the harbour mud and narrow ridges appeared beyond the familiar beach. Soon the villagers could make out something stranger: straight lines, square foundations and the outline of a road leading towards the horizon. The headlines called it a lost city. For sixteen-year-old Nia, whose grandfather had spent his life recording local history, the sight was almost impossible to believe. Her grandfather had often mentioned an old story about bells that could be heard beneath the waves. Until now, she had assumed it was simply a legend.","Nia joined her friend Sol and Dr Mercer, a historian who was visiting the town, on the safe viewing path above the newly exposed remains. They took photographs, recorded distances and compared the structures with eighteenth-century maps. Most maps showed only open water where the buildings stood. One damaged chart, however, included a small symbol shaped like a sunburst and the word “Alderwick”. The name belonged to a settlement that had vanished after a series of terrible floods centuries earlier. Its inhabitants were believed to have moved inland, taking almost nothing with them. Nobody knew where their records had gone.","Nia joined the archaeology team on a walk along the temporary shoreline. The exposed stones were slippery, and the <g word=\"tide\" ph=\"/taɪd/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"The regular rise and fall of the sea.\" ex=\"The tide was very low.\">tide</g> would eventually return, so everyone wore safety helmets and followed the marked path. Dr Mercer explained that the purpose of the visit was to record, not to collect. “A fragment of pottery is valuable because of where it was found,” she said. “Take it away without a record and part of the story disappears.” Nia had hoped to find something beautiful, but the archaeologists were more interested in ordinary things: a doorstep, a cooking pot, and the marks left by worn shoes on stone stairs.","They stopped beside an ancient drain. Its carefully cut channels led towards the sea, suggesting that the residents had tried to control flooding long before the town vanished. Nia took photographs while a student measured the stones. She asked whether Alderwick had been destroyed in a single disaster. Dr Mercer shook her head. “We don't know yet,” she replied. “People may have lived here for generations while the coastline changed slowly.” The answer was less dramatic than the legends Nia had heard, but it made her more curious about the people who had once called the place home.","In the town archive that evening, Nia found a list of family names from the old settlement. Some were still common in Gullhaven. Her own neighbour's surname appeared twice. She began to wonder if Alderwick had not completely disappeared after all. Perhaps its residents had carried their traditions, songs and family stories into nearby communities. The next morning she shared the list with Dr Mercer, who suggested they compare it with the inscriptions on the newly exposed stones. Nia returned to the shoreline with a different idea of what a discovery could be.","From the viewing path, Nia spotted a doorway whose stone frame seemed unusually well <g word=\"preserved\" ph=\"/prɪˈzɜːvd/\" type=\"verb\" def=\"Kept safe or protected from damage.\" ex=\"The old manuscript was preserved.\">preserved</g>. A line of carved symbols ran across the arch: waves, stars and an open hand. Dr Mercer arranged for the area to be examined by specialists before anyone entered. Later that afternoon, the researchers confirmed that the doorway belonged to a public building, possibly a library. Beneath a protective layer of sand, they found an <g word=\"inscription\" ph=\"/ɪnˈskrɪpʃən/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"Words carved or written on a surface.\" ex=\"The inscription was on the stone.\">inscription</g>. Nia helped translate the oldest forms of the place names using her grandfather's notes. The text described a council meeting held after a flood, when the townspeople had decided to move their homes and safeguard the books that recorded their history.","Sol found the story unexpectedly moving. He had imagined the lost city as a place of disaster and mystery, but the inscription suggested something more practical and brave. People had argued, planned and worked together to protect their children and their knowledge. The library had been used until the last possible season, when the community moved to higher ground. The books were apparently carried to a monastery that no longer existed. A few visitors began searching the <g word=\"ruins\" ph=\"/ˈruːɪnz/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"The remains of old buildings.\" ex=\"They studied the ruins carefully.\">ruins</g> for souvenirs, but the researchers quickly reminded everyone that even small objects were <g word=\"evidence\" ph=\"/ˈevɪdəns/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"Facts that support or disprove an explanation.\" ex=\"Scientists looked for evidence.\">evidence</g>. Removing them would make it harder to understand the city's past.","By evening the tide began returning. Water first filled the narrow streets, then covered the mosaic floors, and finally reached the doorway. Gullhaven's residents watched in silence from the cliffs. Nia took one final photograph, not of the stones but of her grandfather looking across the bay. He had always believed that local stories mattered, even when the evidence seemed thin. Now the tide had given them a rare chance to connect memory with history. The research team promised to publish their findings, and Nia offered to help create an exhibition at the town library.","Months later, the exhibition opened. It contained drawings, photographs, maps and the voices of older residents telling the legends they had heard as children. Beside the reconstructed inscription stood a simple statement: “A city is more than its buildings.” Nia had suggested the words herself. She no longer thought of Alderwick as a place swallowed by the sea. She thought of it as a community that had changed, adapted and carried its stories into the future. When the next low tide came, she returned to the shore—not to search for treasure, but to listen."],"glossary":[{"word":"tide","ph":"/taɪd/","type":"noun","def":"The regular rise and fall of the sea.","ex":"The tide was very low."},{"word":"ruins","ph":"/ˈruːɪnz/","type":"noun","def":"The remains of old buildings.","ex":"They studied the ruins carefully."},{"word":"inscription","ph":"/ɪnˈskrɪpʃən/","type":"noun","def":"Words carved or written on a surface.","ex":"The inscription was on the stone."},{"word":"evidence","ph":"/ˈevɪdəns/","type":"noun","def":"Facts that support or disprove an explanation.","ex":"Scientists looked for evidence."},{"word":"preserved","ph":"/prɪˈzɜːvd/","type":"verb","def":"Kept safe or protected from damage.","ex":"The old manuscript was preserved."}],"questions":[{"q":"Why does the hidden city appear?","keywords":["unusually","exposes","streets","foundations","normally"],"model":"An unusually low tide exposes the streets and foundations normally covered by seawater.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : Why does the hidden city appear?","frModel":"Réponse possible : An unusually low tide exposes the streets and foundations normally covered by seawater."},{"q":"What does the inscription reveal?","keywords":["records","inhabitants","chose","protect","knowledge"],"model":"It records how the inhabitants chose to protect knowledge and leave during repeated floods.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : What does the inscription reveal?","frModel":"Réponse possible : It records how the inhabitants chose to protect knowledge and leave during repeated floods."},{"q":"Why do the characters decide not to remove the objects?","keywords":["experts","study","preserve","responsibly","rather"],"model":"They want experts to study and preserve the site responsibly rather than damage it.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : Why do the characters decide not to remove the objects?","frModel":"Réponse possible : They want experts to study and preserve the site responsibly rather than damage it."}],"writingPrompt":"Write 100–140 words about what happens after the end of “The City Beneath the Tide”. Use two words from the glossary.","writingHints":["At the end of the story…","The next morning…","I think the character would…"],"writingKeywords":["tide","ruins","inscription","evidence","preserved"],"strategies":{"skim":{"feedback":"✅ La situation centrale de <em>The City Beneath the Tide</em> peut être résumée en une ou deux phrases.","pistes":["Qui sont les personnages principaux ?","Quel problème doivent-ils résoudre ?","Raconte le dénouement sans détailler toutes les étapes."]},"scan":{"feedback":"✅ Repérer les faits précis demande de chercher les mots-clés plutôt que de tout relire.","pistes":["Cherche un nom propre ou un lieu important.","Repère un événement qui change la situation.","Appuie ta réponse sur un détail précis du texte."]},"infer":{"feedback":"✅ Une bonne inférence s’appuie sur des indices observables dans le texte.","pistes":["Why do the characters decide not to remove the objects?","Quels comportements des personnages peuvent justifier ta réponse ?","Fais la différence entre ce qui est écrit et ce que tu déduis."]},"vocab":{"feedback":"✅ Le contexte aide à comprendre un mot inconnu.","pistes":["Observe les mots qui entourent le terme.","Vérifie ton hypothèse dans le glossaire.","Réutilise le mot dans une phrase personnelle."]},"struct":{"feedback":"✅ Ce récit possède une progression narrative complète.","pistes":["Identifie la situation initiale, le problème, la recherche et la résolution.","Explique comment le suspense se construit.","Le texte comporte 9 paragraphes : donne un court titre à chacun."]},"intent":{"feedback":"✅ Réfléchis au message que l’auteur souhaite transmettre.","pistes":["Quelle décision du personnage fait avancer le récit ?","Comment l’atmosphère évolue-t-elle ?","Quelle idée l’histoire te laisse-t-elle à la fin ?"]}},"longRead":true,"isOriginal":false},{"id":510,"title":"The Orchestra of Forgotten Names","eyebrow":"Contemporary Fiction","level":"B2","levelClass":"b2","source":"Original graded reader · Cozy Storybook","readTime":"6 min","words":873,"preview":"An old concert hall holds the names of musicians nobody remembers, and one student decides to listen.","paragraphs":["The Grand Lyric had once been the most famous concert hall in the city. Now its red velvet seats were faded and the roof leaked whenever it rained. Leila first entered the building because her school <g word=\"orchestra\" ph=\"/ˈɔːkɪstrə/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"A large group of musicians performing together.\" ex=\"The orchestra began to play.\">orchestra</g> had been invited to rehearse there before a charity concert. She was fifteen and played the violin, though she was often too shy to perform a solo. While the other students unpacked their instruments, she wandered into a corridor lined with photographs of composers, conductors and celebrated soloists. Every portrait had a brass nameplate, except for six empty frames near the stairs.","An elderly usher named Mr Vale noticed her looking. He explained that the photographs had gone missing during renovations many years earlier. “No one could agree who belonged in those frames,” he said. Leila found that explanation odd. Surely there were records of everyone who had performed at the hall? Mr Vale led her to a storeroom filled with programmes and <g word=\"rehearsal\" ph=\"/rɪˈhɜːsəl/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"A practice session before a performance.\" ex=\"We had a rehearsal on Thursday.\">rehearsal</g> notebooks. As Leila turned the pages, she noticed that many arrangements were signed with initials rather than full names. Some of the most beautiful passages had been written in the margins by musicians whose contributions were never mentioned in the official programmes.","At home, Leila listened to an old recording of the orchestra. The music was full of bright brass sounds, but she could also hear a quiet melody played by a single violin. The programme named the conductor and the famous soloist, yet it said nothing about the musicians who had created the arrangement. She replayed the passage several times and tried to write down the notes. When her mother asked what she was doing, Leila said she was looking for a person hidden inside the music.","The next day she visited the city archive. A volunteer showed her boxes of concert programmes, newspaper reviews and handwritten letters. The papers smelled of dust and old ink. One letter described an orchestra member who regularly rewrote difficult parts so that younger players could join the concerts. Another mentioned a woman who organised rehearsals when the conductor was away. Their names were absent from the grand posters in the concert hall. Leila began a list in her notebook, carefully recording every source so she could check the information later.","Back at school, she explained her project to the music teacher. He encouraged her to speak with the older musicians who remembered the hall. Some remembered famous performances; others remembered small acts of kindness, late rehearsals and people who repaired instruments after everyone had gone home. Leila realised that the orchestra's history could not be found in one perfect document. It lived in many voices, and some of those voices did not agree. She would need patience, curiosity and a willingness to ask questions without deciding the answers in advance.","Leila's music teacher encouraged her to investigate. Over several weeks she compared handwriting, traced the initials through old letters and contacted families of former orchestra members. Her research revealed a quiet history of overlooked work. A cellist had rewritten difficult passages so younger musicians could play them. A pianist had composed introductions for community concerts. A percussionist had organised free music lessons for children who could not afford instruments. The famous names on the wall were only part of the hall's story. Leila began to wonder whether the upcoming concert could honour the people who had helped make the music possible.","Convincing the orchestra proved harder than she expected. Some students argued that the <g word=\"forgotten\" ph=\"/fəˈɡɒtən/\" type=\"adjective\" def=\"Not remembered any more.\" ex=\"The forgotten song returned.\">forgotten</g> pieces were too unfamiliar; others worried that there was not enough time to prepare. Leila listened to their concerns, then proposed that they perform just one short <g word=\"composition\" ph=\"/ˌkɒmpəˈzɪʃən/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"A piece of music created by someone.\" ex=\"She wrote a new composition.\">composition</g>. She chose a gentle opening movement written by the pianist, whose name she had discovered was Evelyn Marsh. They rehearsed it carefully and gradually came to love its changing melody. On the last afternoon, Mr Vale arrived carrying a small wooden box. Inside were the missing photographs, wrapped in paper. An old label had been attached to the lid: “Contributors—awaiting identification.”","The evening of the concert arrived with a thunderstorm. Rain tapped on the windows as the <g word=\"audience\" ph=\"/ˈɔːdiəns/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"The people watching or listening to a performance.\" ex=\"The audience applauded.\">audience</g> filled the hall. Leila stood in front of her friends, holding a short speech she had rewritten at least twenty times. Her hands were shaking, but when she looked up she saw Mr Vale smiling in the second row. She told the audience about the photographs, the notebooks and Evelyn Marsh. Then the orchestra began to play. The music was delicate at first, almost uncertain, before growing into a bright, generous theme. When it ended, the audience stood and applauded for a long time.","Afterwards, the school helped create a digital archive of the hall's history. The six portraits returned to the corridor with carefully researched names, but the project also included an open invitation for more stories. Leila learnt that <g word=\"recognition\" ph=\"/ˌrekəɡˈnɪʃən/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"Acknowledging someone’s work or achievement.\" ex=\"She received recognition for her work.\">recognition</g> was not simply a matter of giving someone a place on a wall. It meant being willing to look beyond the most obvious names and to acknowledge how many people contribute to something beautiful. At the next rehearsal she still felt nervous when asked to play alone. But she knew that the melody she played would never be hers alone."],"glossary":[{"word":"orchestra","ph":"/ˈɔːkɪstrə/","type":"noun","def":"A large group of musicians performing together.","ex":"The orchestra began to play."},{"word":"audience","ph":"/ˈɔːdiəns/","type":"noun","def":"The people watching or listening to a performance.","ex":"The audience applauded."},{"word":"rehearsal","ph":"/rɪˈhɜːsəl/","type":"noun","def":"A practice session before a performance.","ex":"We had a rehearsal on Thursday."},{"word":"composition","ph":"/ˌkɒmpəˈzɪʃən/","type":"noun","def":"A piece of music created by someone.","ex":"She wrote a new composition."},{"word":"forgotten","ph":"/fəˈɡɒtən/","type":"adjective","def":"Not remembered any more.","ex":"The forgotten song returned."},{"word":"recognition","ph":"/ˌrekəɡˈnɪʃən/","type":"noun","def":"Acknowledging someone’s work or achievement.","ex":"She received recognition for her work."}],"questions":[{"q":"What does Leila discover in the music archive?","keywords":["finds","compositions","musicians","whose","names"],"model":"She finds compositions by musicians whose names were missing from the concert programmes.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : What does Leila discover in the music archive?","frModel":"Réponse possible : She finds compositions by musicians whose names were missing from the concert programmes."},{"q":"Why were the names forgotten?","keywords":["records","credited","performers","overlooked","contributors"],"model":"Old records credited only the lead performers and overlooked many contributors.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : Why were the names forgotten?","frModel":"Réponse possible : Old records credited only the lead performers and overlooked many contributors."},{"q":"What changes at the final concert?","keywords":["orchestra","acknowledges","overlooked","musicians","performs"],"model":"The full orchestra acknowledges the overlooked musicians and performs their music publicly.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : What changes at the final concert?","frModel":"Réponse possible : The full orchestra acknowledges the overlooked musicians and performs their music publicly."}],"writingPrompt":"Write 100–140 words about what happens after the end of “The Orchestra of Forgotten Names”. Use two words from the glossary.","writingHints":["At the end of the story…","The next morning…","I think the character would…"],"writingKeywords":["orchestra","audience","rehearsal","composition","forgotten"],"strategies":{"skim":{"feedback":"✅ La situation centrale de <em>The Orchestra of Forgotten Names</em> peut être résumée en une ou deux phrases.","pistes":["Qui sont les personnages principaux ?","Quel problème doivent-ils résoudre ?","Raconte le dénouement sans détailler toutes les étapes."]},"scan":{"feedback":"✅ Repérer les faits précis demande de chercher les mots-clés plutôt que de tout relire.","pistes":["Cherche un nom propre ou un lieu important.","Repère un événement qui change la situation.","Appuie ta réponse sur un détail précis du texte."]},"infer":{"feedback":"✅ Une bonne inférence s’appuie sur des indices observables dans le texte.","pistes":["What changes at the final concert?","Quels comportements des personnages peuvent justifier ta réponse ?","Fais la différence entre ce qui est écrit et ce que tu déduis."]},"vocab":{"feedback":"✅ Le contexte aide à comprendre un mot inconnu.","pistes":["Observe les mots qui entourent le terme.","Vérifie ton hypothèse dans le glossaire.","Réutilise le mot dans une phrase personnelle."]},"struct":{"feedback":"✅ Ce récit possède une progression narrative complète.","pistes":["Identifie la situation initiale, le problème, la recherche et la résolution.","Explique comment le suspense se construit.","Le texte comporte 9 paragraphes : donne un court titre à chacun."]},"intent":{"feedback":"✅ Réfléchis au message que l’auteur souhaite transmettre.","pistes":["Quelle décision du personnage fait avancer le récit ?","Comment l’atmosphère évolue-t-elle ?","Quelle idée l’histoire te laisse-t-elle à la fin ?"]}},"longRead":true,"isOriginal":false},{"id":511,"title":"The Cartographer's Promise","eyebrow":"Fantasy","level":"B2","levelClass":"b2","source":"Original graded reader · Cozy Storybook","readTime":"6 min","words":900,"preview":"A map that changes whenever someone tells the truth leads two friends towards a village missing from every atlas.","paragraphs":["Rowan inherited two things from her grandfather: a brass <g word=\"compass\" ph=\"/ˈkʌmpəs/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"A tool showing directions.\" ex=\"We checked our compass on the walk.\">compass</g> that never seemed to point north, and a map with an empty space in the middle. He had been a <g word=\"cartographer\" ph=\"/kɑːˈtɒɡrəfə/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"A person who makes maps.\" ex=\"The cartographer drew the coast.\">cartographer</g>, a maker of maps, and his notebooks filled the attic of their seaside cottage. Every coastline was carefully drawn, except on the final map, where a white circle interrupted the hills. Beneath it were the words, “No road is found by pretending.” Rowan assumed it was one of his jokes until she tried to trace the circle with a pencil. The ink moved beneath her hand, forming the outline of a narrow path across the cliffs.","Her friend Idris was the first person she told. He thought the ink might be reacting to heat, so they tested it with a lamp. Nothing changed. They tried the compass, which spun twice and settled towards the empty circle. Then Idris admitted that he had hidden one of Rowan's grandfather's letters after accidentally tearing its envelope. The map shivered and a second path appeared. Both friends stared. Slowly they understood that the map responded not to magic words but to honest ones. Whenever someone spoke a truth they had been avoiding, the <g word=\"landscape\" ph=\"/ˈlændskeɪp/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"The appearance of land in an area.\" ex=\"The landscape changed after the rain.\">landscape</g> became a little clearer.","Rowan spread the changing map across the kitchen table. The paper was old and thin, but the ink looked fresh. She and Idris compared it with a modern atlas. The coastlines were familiar, yet several roads on her grandfather's map did not exist anywhere else. At the centre was a white space shaped like a valley. Whenever Rowan tried to draw its outline, the pencil marks disappeared. Idris suggested that the map might be damaged, but Rowan noticed that the empty space seemed to move when the room grew quiet.","They decided to test their theory about <g word=\"honesty\" ph=\"/ˈɒnɪsti/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"Being truthful and sincere.\" ex=\"Honesty helped rebuild trust.\">honesty</g>. Idris admitted that he had lost an expensive library book and had been afraid to tell the librarian. A narrow line appeared on the map, leading towards a bridge. Rowan confessed that she had hidden her grandfather's final letter because she did not want to accept that he was gone. The line grew into a winding path. Neither confession was easy. They both felt embarrassed, but afterwards they also felt lighter. Rowan understood that the map did not reward people for being perfect. It responded when they stopped pretending.","Before leaving, they visited the library together. Idris explained what had happened to the missing book and offered to replace it. The librarian appreciated his honesty and suggested a way to help repair the old binding instead. Rowan took her grandfather's letter from a drawer and read it properly for the first time. He had written that a good cartographer should listen more than she spoke, and should never confuse an empty place on a page with a place where nobody lived. The words made the blank valley feel less mysterious, and more like a <g word=\"promise\" ph=\"/ˈprɒmɪs/\" type=\"noun\" def=\"A firm statement that you will do something.\" ex=\"He kept his promise to call.\">promise</g> they still needed to understand.","The following morning they followed the path in the map along the cliffs. Mist covered the sea, and the sound of waves made conversation difficult. The compass pointed towards an old lighthouse that had been closed for years. At its door they met a woman named Ada, who had known Rowan's grandfather. She explained that the map led to Havenfold, a village omitted from official charts after its residents had refused to abandon one another during a season of terrible storms. Over time, outsiders had told different stories about why the village had disappeared. Some called its people foolish; others claimed the place had never existed. The truth was more complicated.","Ada invited the teenagers into the lighthouse and showed them a series of letters written by Havenfold's residents. Some described brave rescues, others ordinary disagreements about food, repairs and where families should live. Rowan expected a perfect community hidden in the mist. Instead, she discovered a group of people who had made mistakes, argued, apologised and kept trying to care for one another. Her grandfather had promised not to reveal their home until the village was ready to decide how it wanted to be known. The map was not simply a treasure guide. It was a reminder that telling the truth required responsibility.","When Rowan and Idris finally reached Havenfold, they found a small harbour, stone cottages and a school decorated with painted maps. The residents welcomed them cautiously. They were worried that visitors might turn their village into a curiosity rather than respect its history. Rowan listened before showing them the blank circle on her grandfather's chart. She offered to return the map and let the villagers decide what to do with it. After a long meeting, the council agreed that the location could be recorded for historians and emergency services, while personal stories would remain private unless their owners wished to share them.","Rowan drew the new chart with the help of Havenfold's children. The compass finally pointed north, and the white circle faded into a pattern of roads, gardens and paths. At the bottom of the page Rowan added a note in her own handwriting: “A map can tell us where a place is, but only people can explain what it means.” She rolled it carefully and handed it to Ada. On the walk home, Idris asked whether the map would ever change again. Rowan smiled. “Of course,” she said. “So will we.”"],"glossary":[{"word":"cartographer","ph":"/kɑːˈtɒɡrəfə/","type":"noun","def":"A person who makes maps.","ex":"The cartographer drew the coast."},{"word":"compass","ph":"/ˈkʌmpəs/","type":"noun","def":"A tool showing directions.","ex":"We checked our compass on the walk."},{"word":"landscape","ph":"/ˈlændskeɪp/","type":"noun","def":"The appearance of land in an area.","ex":"The landscape changed after the rain."},{"word":"honesty","ph":"/ˈɒnɪsti/","type":"noun","def":"Being truthful and sincere.","ex":"Honesty helped rebuild trust."},{"word":"promise","ph":"/ˈprɒmɪs/","type":"noun","def":"A firm statement that you will do something.","ex":"He kept his promise to call."}],"questions":[{"q":"What makes the map unusual?","keywords":["coastline","changes","someone","speaks","honestly"],"model":"Its coastline changes when someone speaks honestly about something they have been hiding.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : What makes the map unusual?","frModel":"Réponse possible : Its coastline changes when someone speaks honestly about something they have been hiding."},{"q":"What does the hidden village represent?","keywords":["refuge","built","people","chose","cooperation"],"model":"It is a refuge built by people who chose cooperation and care rather than being forgotten.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : What does the hidden village represent?","frModel":"Réponse possible : It is a refuge built by people who chose cooperation and care rather than being forgotten."},{"q":"How does Rowan keep the promise?","keywords":["shares","village","history","responsibly","instead"],"model":"She shares the map and the village’s history responsibly instead of claiming it as her own discovery.","frQ":"Réponds en anglais à la question : How does Rowan keep the promise?","frModel":"Réponse possible : She shares the map and the village’s history responsibly instead of claiming it as her own discovery."}],"writingPrompt":"Write 100–140 words about what happens after the end of “The Cartographer's Promise”. Use two words from the glossary.","writingHints":["At the end of the story…","The next morning…","I think the character would…"],"writingKeywords":["cartographer","compass","landscape","honesty","boundary"],"strategies":{"skim":{"feedback":"✅ La situation centrale de <em>The Cartographer&#x27;s Promise</em> peut être résumée en une ou deux phrases.","pistes":["Qui sont les personnages principaux ?","Quel problème doivent-ils résoudre ?","Raconte le dénouement sans détailler toutes les étapes."]},"scan":{"feedback":"✅ Repérer les faits précis demande de chercher les mots-clés plutôt que de tout relire.","pistes":["Cherche un nom propre ou un lieu important.","Repère un événement qui change la situation.","Appuie ta réponse sur un détail précis du texte."]},"infer":{"feedback":"✅ Une bonne inférence s’appuie sur des indices observables dans le texte.","pistes":["How does Rowan keep the promise?","Quels comportements des personnages peuvent justifier ta réponse ?","Fais la différence entre ce qui est écrit et ce que tu déduis."]},"vocab":{"feedback":"✅ Le contexte aide à comprendre un mot inconnu.","pistes":["Observe les mots qui entourent le terme.","Vérifie ton hypothèse dans le glossaire.","Réutilise le mot dans une phrase personnelle."]},"struct":{"feedback":"✅ Ce récit possède une progression narrative complète.","pistes":["Identifie la situation initiale, le problème, la recherche et la résolution.","Explique comment le suspense se construit.","Le texte comporte 9 paragraphes : donne un court titre à chacun."]},"intent":{"feedback":"✅ Réfléchis au message que l’auteur souhaite transmettre.","pistes":["Quelle décision du personnage fait avancer le récit ?","Comment l’atmosphère évolue-t-elle ?","Quelle idée l’histoire te laisse-t-elle à la fin ?"]}},"longRead":true,"isOriginal":false}]);
